## Is kidney disease a disability?

Under the **Equality Act 2010**, a condition counts as a disability if it has a **substantial** (more than minor) and **long-term** (12 months or more) effect on your normal daily activities. The law looks at how you would be **without treatment**. Mild CKD may not be covered, but advanced CKD, dialysis and a transplant usually are.

## At work

Reasonable adjustments could include:

- flexible hours to fit around **dialysis**
- time off for appointments, and after a transplant
- more breaks, and less physical work, if you are tired
- working from home

## Benefits

PIP is based on how CKD affects you on most days. Advanced CKD and dialysis can affect activities such as:

- **managing treatments**, if you need help with **home dialysis**. Points depend on how many hours a week of help you need
- **preparing food**, **washing** and **moving around**, if you are very tired or breathless

{% checker tool="pip" /%}

If you are over State Pension age, look at [Attendance Allowance](/benefits/attendance-allowance/) instead of PIP. It is for people who need help with personal care or supervision.

## If you cannot work

If your health means you cannot work, or can only work a little, you may be able to get [New Style Employment and Support Allowance](/benefits/esa/) or extra money in [Universal Credit](/benefits/universal-credit-health/). These are separate from PIP, and you can get them at the same time.

## Travel to dialysis

You may be able to get **NHS patient transport** to dialysis, or help with travel costs through the **Healthcare Travel Costs Scheme** if you get certain benefits or are on a low income. See [help with travel and other health costs](/support/health-costs/travel-and-other-costs/).

Free, independent advisers, such as **Citizens Advice** or a local welfare rights service, can help you check what you can claim and fill in forms.
